# Erdős Problem #99 — matched `g=0.0050` loaded-slack externalization with penetration matching

**Date:** 2026-09-15  
**Target:** C-II, canonical contact-minimal full-core Type-II branch  
**Dependencies:** loaded-degree-slack source externalization; matched iterative-pruning gap `g=0.0050`; Section-171 `epsilon=0.0023374`; shallow-tooth penetration matching and deep purity  
**Claim class:** proof-only source-level composition awaiting independent audit  
**External literature:** no new import

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

The loaded-slack externalization theorem removes the abstract loaded contact-degree
currency `s_B`, then pays the remaining loaded flat-pentagon centers by singleton-train
capacity.  Its original tooth conversion predated the penetration-matching theorem and
therefore used the coarse bound

\[
N_2\le4E_{pen}(\eta)+{d_{out}\over\eta}.
\]

The later finite geometry removes that factor four.  For every admissible fixed
`eta`, the `eta`-shallow teeth have pairwise-distinct apexes and pairwise-distinct deep
endpoints, so their selected internal penetration contacts form a matching.  Hence

\[
\boxed{
N_2\le E_{pen}(\eta)+{d_{out}\over\eta}+o(D).
}
\tag{EM.1}

Combining (EM.1) with the loaded-slack theorem and the matched atomic endpoint gives a
substantially sharper two-currency reduction:

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {E_{pen}(\eta)+4|E_{att}|\over D}
\ge C_g^{src}(t,\eta),
}
\tag{EM.2}

where

\[
C_g^{src}(t,\eta)
=13\pi+7C_g-29t
+{1\over2}\left(11-{1\over\eta}\right)R_g(t),
\tag{EM.3}
\]

\[
C_g=2\sqrt{\Delta_2}+g,
\qquad
R_g(t)={9t\over2}-2\pi-C_g.
\tag{EM.4}

The two edge families are physically disjoint:

- `E_pen(eta)` consists of unloaded-to-unloaded internal contacts;
- `E_att` consists of loaded-to-unloaded contacts.

Thus (EM.2) also gives

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {|E_{pen}(\eta)\cup E_{att}|\over D}
\ge {C_g^{src}(t,\eta)\over4},
}
\tag{EM.5}

and the sharper equal-branch alternative

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}\max\{E_{pen}(\eta),|E_{att}|\}
\ge {C_g^{src}(t,\eta)\over5}.
}
\tag{EM.6}

The denominator five, rather than eight or ten, is the main quantitative gain.

At the independently audited matched endpoint

\[
g=0.0050,
\qquad
C_{146}={4\over9}\left(\pi+\sqrt{\Delta_2}\right),
\qquad
t_{50}=C_{146}+0.0023374,
\]

choose the exact rational threshold

\[
\eta={17\over250}=0.068.
\]

It satisfies both

\[
\eta<{1\over11}
\]

and the shallow-tooth locality requirement

\[
\eta<2(1-\cos(\pi/12)).
\]

Then

\[
R_g(t_{50})=0.00551830
\tag{EM.7}
\]

and

\[
\boxed{
C_{0.005}^{src}(t_{50},17/250)
=1.3641815060647008748\ldots .
}
\tag{EM.8}

Consequently

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {|E_{pen}(17/250)\cup E_{att}|\over D}
\ge0.3410453765161752187\ldots,
}
\tag{EM.9}

and, more usefully,

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {1\over D}\max\{E_{pen}(17/250),|E_{att}|\}
\ge0.2728363012129401749\ldots .
}
\tag{EM.10}

Thus every small-residual low-`t` survivor contains either

1. an internal unloaded-to-unloaded **penetration matching** of size at least
   `0.2728363D-o(D)`; or
2. at least `0.2728363D-o(D)` distinct loaded-to-unloaded attachment contacts.

No loaded degree slack, tooth count, or loaded-flat-center count remains in the final
alternative.

---

## 1. Start from finite loaded-slack externalization

The source-externalization theorem proves

\[
N_2+2|L_{flat}^{bd}|+4|E_{att}|
+4B_o+12X+{10R_F\over\theta_*}+16
\ge3q-b.
\tag{EM.11}

After bridge and degree-excess payments and on `R_F=o(D)`, this becomes

\[
\liminf {N_2+2|L_{flat}^{bd}|+4|E_{att}|\over D}
\ge A_g(t)+9\delta+6\kappa,
\tag{EM.12}
\]

where

\[
A_g(t)=11\pi+6C_g-24t,
\qquad
\delta={b-P_F\over D},
\qquad
\kappa={k\over D}.
\]

The theorem also gives the singleton-train capacity bound

\[
\limsup {|L_{flat}^{bd}|\over D}
\le {5t\over2}-\pi-\delta-\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{g\over2}.
\tag{EM.13}

---

## 2. Replace the tooth currency without multiplicity loss

The tooth detour identity gives

\[
N_{sh}(\eta)
\ge N_2-{d_{out}\over\eta}.
\tag{EM.14}

The penetration-matching theorem produces exactly one distinct internal contact edge
per shallow tooth, and the selected edges form a matching.  Therefore

\[
E_{pen}(\eta)=N_{sh}(\eta)
\]

for a suitable selected penetration matching, so (EM.14) is equivalent to (EM.1).

Insert (EM.1) into (EM.12):

\[
\liminf {E_{pen}(\eta)+2|L_{flat}^{bd}|+4|E_{att}|\over D}
\ge A_g(t)+\left(9-{1\over\eta}\right)\delta+6\kappa.
\tag{EM.15}

Now subtract twice the upper bound (EM.13).  This yields

\[
\boxed{
\liminf {E_{pen}(\eta)+4|E_{att}|\over D}
\ge
13\pi+7C_g-29t
+\left(11-{1\over\eta}\right)\delta+6\kappa.
}
\tag{EM.16}

For `eta<1/11`, the coefficient of `delta` is negative.  The exact topology envelope

\[
2\delta+{\kappa\over2}\le R_g(t)
\tag{EM.17}
\]

therefore minimizes the right side at `kappa=0`, `delta=R_g(t)/2`, proving
(EM.2)--(EM.4).

---

## 3. Exact disjointness and the branch constants

Every selected penetration edge lies entirely inside one unloaded positive-contact
component.  In particular it has two unloaded endpoints.  Every edge in `E_att` has
one loaded and one unloaded endpoint by construction.  Hence

\[
E_{pen}(\eta)\cap E_{att}=\varnothing.
\tag{EM.18}

Let

\[
p={E_{pen}(\eta)\over D},
\qquad
a={|E_{att}|\over D},
\qquad C=C_g^{src}(t,\eta).
\]

Equation (EM.2) is

\[
p+4a\ge C.
\]

Since the edge classes are disjoint,

\[
|E_{pen}\cup E_{att}|/D=p+a.
\]

The minimum of `p+a` subject to `p,a>=0` and `p+4a>=C` is `C/4`, proving (EM.5).
If both `p<C/5` and `a<C/5`, then `p+4a<C`, contradiction.  This proves (EM.6).

The penetration branch is stronger than a raw edge population: because the selected
penetration edges form a matching, `pD` such edges use `2pD` distinct unloaded
vertices, and the deep endpoints have no loaded contact neighbors and internal degree
three to five.

---

## 4. Matched numerical endpoint

For `g=0.0050` and `t=t_50`, cancellation in (EM.4) gives exactly

\[
R_g(t_{50})
={9\over2}(0.0023374)-0.0050
=0.00551830.
\tag{EM.19}

Using `eta=17/250`, the companion verifier checks

\[
C_g^{src}=1.3641815060647008748\ldots,
\]

\[
C_g^{src}/4=0.3410453765161752187\ldots,
\]

and

\[
C_g^{src}/5=0.2728363012129401749\ldots .
\]

The locality condition is checked from the exact identity

\[
2(1-\cos(\pi/12))
=2-{\sqrt6+\sqrt2\over2}
>0.068.
\]

---

## 5. Frontier effect

The small-residual low-`t` branch now has a clean physical two-way obstruction:

```text
(A) >= 0.2728363 D internal penetration matching edges,
    using >= 0.5456726 D distinct unloaded vertices,
    with attachment-free deep endpoints;

or

(B) >= 0.2728363 D distinct loaded-to-unloaded attachment contacts.
```

The disjoint union always contains at least `0.3410453D` actual unit contacts.

This is substantially sharper than the original loaded-slack theorem, whose final
union floor lost both the old tooth multiplicity factor and a possible overlap factor.
The next useful closing work can now separate into two concrete capacity problems:

- continue the penetration matching inward (the second-layer theorem already starts
  this); or
- prove an attachment-incidence / loaded-boundary capacity theorem for branch (B).

No positive KKT-weight floor is asserted.  C-II and Level I remain open.

## 6. Scope audit

- The normalized theorem inherits the `R_F=o(D)` low-`t` full-core Type-II scope of
  loaded-slack externalization.
- The penetration matching theorem is finite and retains all source points.
- `E_pen` and `E_att` are disjoint by endpoint type, not by an asymptotic argument.
- `eta=17/250` lies strictly inside both required fixed thresholds.
- `g=0.0050` and the corresponding Section-171 floor are independently audited.
- Contact-isolated source points remain in the full source.
- No exact unit triangle or strict same-cardinality sub-`D` competitor is produced.
